# Untitled

Hydrogen Storage in a Potassium-Ion-Bound Metal–Organic Framework Incorporating Crown Ether Struts as Specific Cation Binding Sites**
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted considerable attention because of their various potential applications such as hydrogen storage,[1] carbon dioxide capture,[2] gas separation,[3] catalysis,[4] and fabrication of nanoparticles.[5] In particular, MOFs have been considered to be candidate materials for hydrogen storage, even though their practical applications are still limited as a result of unsolved problems, such as low gas-storage capacity at ambient temperature and low stability against water. To enhance \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) storage capacities of MOFs at ambient temperature, the interaction energies between the frameworks and \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) gas molecules should be increased. In general, MOFs interact with the guest molecules included in the pores through weak nonspecific interactions. To generate size-selective guest-binding properties or to induce strong interactions with the gas adsorbate, studies involving the control of pore aperture
size and shape,[6] functionalization of the ligands,[7] and creation of vacant coordination sites on metal ions[8] have been conducted. The introduction of a guest-specific binding site or active domain has proven to be difficult, and there has been only one report to date, a pseudorotaxane-type MOF.[9] The MOF had organic struts attaching a 34- or 36-membered macrocyclic polyether pendant and it bound a paraquat dication \((\mathrm{POT}^{2+})\) guest by host–guest interactions. We previously revealed that the inclusion of specific guest molecules, 18-crown-6 and 15-crown-5, in the pores of a MOF provided an electrostatic field to enhance the binding energy of the \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) gas molecules.[10] In addition, the impregnation of cations in a MOF has been shown to increase the gas-storage properties of the MOF.[2b] However, the selective inclusion of a desired cation in a MOF is still a challenging problem.
Herein, we report a MOF incorporating a 18-crown-6 (18Cr6) moiety as a framework component, \(\mathrm{[Zn_{5}(\upmu_{3}-}\) \(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (TBADB-18Cr6)-4DMF]-13DMF- \(12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (SNU-200; TBADB-18Cr6 \(=4,4^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}\) -terabenzoic acid dibenzo-18- crown-6, \(\mathrm{DMF}\,{=}\,\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}\) -dimethylformamide), which provides specific guest binding sites for \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\) , \(\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}\) , and methyl viologen \((\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+})\) cations. Upon binding of these cations, the MOF underwent a single-crystal to single-crystal transformation, and the X-ray crystal structures of the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) and \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) bound samples were able to be determined. The structure of the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+}\) bound MOF was characterized by "locate simulations" and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns. SNU-200 and the cation bound samples, after activation with supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , adsorb \(\Nu_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gases. The gas-sorption properties of the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) bound sample were compared with those of \(\mathrm{SNU}{-}200^{\prime}\) (prime stands for sample activated by using supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) ) and the \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) bound MOF as well as the higher charged organic cation \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+}\) bound MOF. Contrary to the previously reported MOFs,[11] the surface areas of the cation/counteranion guest bound \((\mathrm{K}^{+},\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+},\mathrm{MV}^{2+}/\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{SCN^{-}}\) ) samples do not decrease compared to that of SNU-200'. SNU-200' exhibits enhanced isosteric heat \((Q_{\mathrm{st}})\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption compared to common Zn-MOFs.[12] Among \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) , \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) , and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+}\) bound SNU-200' analogues, the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) ion bound MOF shows the most highly enhanced isosteric heat \((Q_{\mathrm{st}})\) of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption.
To our knowledge, this is the first report on the gas-adsorption properties of a MOF incorporating a crown ether moiety as the framework component. Although there have been several reports for the synthesis of rotaxane-type MOFs,[13] they were incapable of adsorbing any gas molecules.
Colorless crystals of SNU-200 have been synthesized by heating a mixture of \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) and \( \mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBADB}-18\mathrm{Cr}6 \)
in \(\mathrm{DMF/H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The X-ray crystal structure of SNU-200 exhibits a doubly interpenetrated 3D network generating 1D channels \((8.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) that extend along the \(c\)-axis (Figure 1). In a pore, two 18Cr6 face each other (Figure 2). There are three crystallographically independent \(\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers \((\mathrm{Zn}1,\mathrm{Zn}2,\) and \(\mathrm{Zn}3)\), and they are linked by an \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)bridge as well as eight different carboxylates to form a \(\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\) cluster unit with the inversion center at \(\mathrm{Zn}3\) (Supporting Information, Figure S1). The \(\mathrm{Zn}1\) and \(\mathrm{Zn}3\) have an octahedral coordination geometry \([\mathrm{\check{A}O{-}Z n1{-}O}\), av. 89.63(4); \([\mathrm{\check{A}O{-}Z n3{-}O}\), av. 89.85(4)] and \(\mathrm{Zn}2\) has a tetrahedral geometry \([\mathrm{\check{A}O{-}Z n2{-}O}\), av. 109.66(6)]. The \(\mathrm{Zn}1\) atom is coordinated with two DMF solvent molecules. The solvent-accessible void volume estimated by PLATON is \(39.2\,\%\) of the whole structure. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) data reveal a \(41.0\,\%\) weight loss at \(25{-}300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which corresponds to the loss of all the coordinated DMF and guest solvent molecules (calcd \(41.75\,\%\) for 13DMF and \(12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)), and no chemical decomposition up to \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Supporting Information, Figure S2).
To see the specific cation binding in SNU-200, the crystals of SNU-200 were immersed in DMF solutions of \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\) \((0.1\mathrm{M})\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) \((0.01\mathrm{M})\), and methyl viologen dichloride \((\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+}2\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}^{-}\), 0.01M), respectively, at room temperature for 3 days. \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\)ions have high binding constants for \(18\mathrm{Cr}6\).[14] We expected that \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{V}^{2+}\) would also interact with 18Cr6 strongly because it is electron deficient. KSCN was chosen as a \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)source instead of KCl because of the easily identifiable \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)ion in the IR spectra, which would clearly indicate if it is coordinated at the metal ion or simply included in the pores of the MOFs. Furthermore, the solubility of KCl in DMF was too low \((2{-}7\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}})\) while \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}\) and \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) have solubilities high enough to satisfy the reaction conditions. After immersion, the extra salts included in the pores of the samples and adsorbed on the surface of the particles were removed by soaking the resulting crystals in fresh DMF for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). During the cation inclusion, SNU-200 underwent single-crystal to single-crystal transformations, and the X-ray crystal structures of \([\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{SNU}{-}200{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\cdot\mathrm{G}\) and \([\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{SNU}{-}200]\cdot\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\cdot\mathrm{G}\) (G, guest solvents) could be determined.
The X-ray structure of \([\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{SNU}{-}200{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\cdot\mathrm{G}\) indicates that \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)ion is located 0.0764(0.0061) Å above the mean plane
made of the six oxygen atoms of 18Cr6 and it is coordinated with two water molecules at the axial sites (Figure 2, Figure S4).[15] In addition, one \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)ion replaces two DMF molecules and one carboxy oxygen atom coordinated at \(\mathrm{Zn}1\) of the pristine MOF, which alters the coordination geometry of \(\mathrm{Zn}1\) from octahedral to tetrahedral \([\mathrm{\check{A}O{-}Z n1{-}O}\), av. 111.46 (0.13); \(\mathrm{\ddot{\A}O{-}Z n1{-}O}\), av. 107.66 (0.19)] (Figure S1). The \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)coordinates to \(\mathrm{Zn}1\) through a \(\mathrm{N}\) atom as confirmed by a \(\mathbf{V}_{\mathrm{CN}}\)
band appearing at \(2087\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in the IR spectrum (Figure S3). On activation of the framework with supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fluid, two water molecules bound at each \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)ion were removed as evidenced by the IR spectra (Figure S5).
In the \(\mathbf{X}\) -ray structure of \([\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200]\cdot\mathrm{Cl}^{-}{\cdot}\mathrm{G},\) , the coordination modes of the \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) ions in the \(Z\mathfrak{n}_{5}\) cluster are the same as those of SNU-200, and each \(Z\mathfrak{n}1\) still coordinates two DMF molecules. The \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ion is positioned 1.577 (0.035) Å above the mean plane of \(18\mathrm{Cr}6\) . The \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ion forms hydrogen bonds with the oxygen atoms of the crown ether with an average \(\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{O}\) distance of 2.949 (0.067) Å. The \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) counteranions are found in the channels. The elemental analysis data indicate that there are \(1.09\,\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) molecules per \(18\mathrm{Cr}6\) , implying that all the \(18\mathrm{Cr}6\) moieties in the MOF bind \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ions.
In \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200]\cdot2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}{\cdot}\mathrm{G},\) , SNU-200 also maintains single crystallinity after the immersion in a DMF solution of methyl viologen (ca. 0.01M). However, even with the synchrotron \(\mathbf{X}\) -ray diffraction data collected at \(100\,\mathrm{K}\) , the included methyl viologen species could not be located because of thermal disorder. Therefore, a “locate simulation” was performed using a sorption module of Materials Studio.[16] The Metropolis Monte Carlo method was chosen for the calculation of the global minimum locations. Universal force field (UFF) was selected for the energy calculation, and the charge equilibration \((Q_{\mathrm{Eq}})\) method was used for the calculation of point atomic charges. The results indicate that \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\) resides beside the 18-crown-6 moiety of SNU-200 (Figure 2), and a maximum of 4 methyl viologen molecules could be accommodated per unit cell, that is, one molecule per formula unit of SNU-200 (Supporting Information).
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns also indicate that the structure of SNU-200 is maintained even after the inclusion of KSCN, \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}\) , and methyl viologen dichloride inside or near the crown ether moiety (Figure S6). In particular, the PXRD pattern for the simulated structure of \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\) loaded SNU-200 is coincident with the measured PXRD data of \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200]\cdot2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}{\cdot}\mathrm{G},\) , and the new peaks at the low-angle regions must correspond to the \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\) guest molecules. After activation, however, the PXRD patterns of SNU-200′ and \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200]\cdot2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) are slightly broadened together with the shift of the (101) peaks to a higher angle region (Figure S7), indicating shrinkage of the frameworks. Interestingly, the PXRD patterns for the activated samples of \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) and \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) bound MOFs show better crystalline properties than the activated SNU-200 and \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200]\cdot2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) . We assume that this occurs because the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) and \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ions are bound within the 18Cr6 moieties, which make the framework more rigid upon activation compared to the other samples. All activated compounds are air sensitive, as
evidenced by their PXRD patterns, which are highly broadened on exposure to air for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Figure S7).
To verify the porosity of the samples activated with supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fluid, the adsorption–desorption isotherms of [SNU-200], \([\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\) , \([\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) , and \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) were measured for \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gases at various temperatures (Table 1). The \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\) gas sorption isotherms of all samples show a type I curve, characteristic of the microporous materials (Figure S8). Contrary to other common MOFs,[11] the BET surface areas of the compounds including cations/counteranions are higher than that of SNU-200. This is because during the activation, the MOFs incorporating the cation bound 18Cr6 moieties together with the counteranion inclusions shrink less, than SNU-200.
Low pressure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms were measured at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(87\,\mathrm{K}\) under \(1\,\mathrm{atm}\) (Figure 3). SNU-200′ uptakes \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas up to \(1.06\;\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(0.72\,\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) at \(87\,\mathrm{K}\) . It shows the
enhanced isosteric heat \((Q_{\mathrm{st}},\,7.70\,\mathrm{kJ}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1})\) of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption compared to those of common Zn-MOFs \((4.1-\) \(6.2\,\mathrm{kJ}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1})\) .[12] Interestingly, among the samples, \([\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\) shows the highest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacity, \(1.19\,\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(0.78\,\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) at \(87\,\mathrm{K}\) , although its surface area is smaller than that of \([\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) . The zero-coverage isosteric heat of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption in \([\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\) , which is estimated from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms measured at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(87\,\mathrm{K}\) by using the virial equation, increases to \(9.92\,\mathrm{kJ}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) from \(7.70\,\mathrm{kJ}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) of SNU-200′ (Table 1, Figure 3). This enhance
ment (by \(2.22\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\)) by the inclusion of the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)ion in the present MOF is much higher than the (by ca. \(1.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\)) previously reported values for the MOFs that included alkali-metal or alkali-earth-metal ions in the pores, which still coordinated the solvent molecules.[17] It is comparable to those of the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) based MOFs.[18] The enhancement of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption energy in \([\mathrm{K^{+}}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\) is attributed to the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) ion having accessible open sites that were generated by the removal of the water molecules bound at \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) ion upon activation of the sample. Although it was reported that the type of anions coordinated at the metal center of a MOF greatly affected the binding energy of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule,[1c] an anion effect on the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values is not observed in the present case. At high pressure (70 bar) and \(298\,\mathrm{K}\), the amount \((0.28\,\mathrm{wt\,\%})\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorbed in \([\mathrm{K^{+}}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\) is slightly higher than that \((0.26\,\mathrm{wt\,\%})\) of \(\mathrm{SNU}{-}200^{\prime}\) (Figure S10). The best MOF for hydrogen storage reported to date, \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{HTBC})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy})]\), has an \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacity of \(1.2\,\mathrm{wt\,\%}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) and 72 bar with the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) value of \(8.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\).[19] In this respect, the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) value of the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) bound SNU-200 represents a significant finding. However, further work must be done to develop MOFs that have both high hydrogen uptake capacities at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) and high \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values.
To determine the position of an adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule in \([\mathrm{K^{+}}\mathrm{CSNU}{-}200^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}^{-}]\), as-synthesized sample, we performed Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) calculations by using a sorption module of Materials Studio (for detailed information, see Supporting Information).[16] The theoretical results indicate that a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule is located above the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) ion in the crown ether moiety near the metal cluster, while it is positioned beside the metal clusters in the \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\) and \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\) bound MOFs (Figure S11).
In conclusion, a MOF incorporating a 18Cr6 crown ether moiety in the strut provides specific/selective binding sites for \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\), and \(\mathrm{MV}^{2+}\) ions. Pristine SNU-200' has a higher isosteric heat of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption than the general Zn type MOFs. Among the cation/counteranion bound MOFs, the \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) bound MOF exhibits the highest \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) value of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption \((9.92\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})\) as a result of the accessible open metal sites on \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\), which are generated during activation. This work presents a significant step forward in tailoring MOFs to accommodate specific cations, which ultimately enhances the hydrogen-storage properties of this material.
Keywords: cation inclusion \(\cdot\) crown ethers \(\cdot\) hydrogen storage \(\cdot\) metal–organic frameworks \(\cdot\) potassium ion
